flowchart TB
    accTitle: How GitHub comes to know every workflow of a new repository
    accDescr: GitHub starts a workflow only once it has registered it, and the first push of a new repository may register none; a run registers its workflow, so those that only a schedule, a release or a person starts stay unknown. After its push, python3 .github/blueprint.py create asks GitHub for up to a minute whether it knows every workflow of .github/workflows/. For each that it doesn't, create runs python3 .github/blueprint.py workflows register, which opens a pull request of one commit that GitHub signs, with a comment at the end of each such workflow. Its merge is a push that changes them, which registers them. Run again, the command opens the pull request that takes the comments out, and the files are the blueprint's again. Then every workflow starts on its push, its schedule, its release or by hand.

    push(["`**blueprint.py create**<br>the first push`"])
    look["`**create looks for up to a minute**<br>Does GitHub know every workflow<br>of .github/workflows/?`"]
    register["`**workflows register**<br>a pull request of one signed commit:<br>a comment at the end of each unknown workflow`"]
    merge(["You: merge it<br>once its checks are green"])
    unmark["`**workflows register again**<br>the pull request that takes<br>the comments out`"]
    again(["You: merge it"])
    known["`**Every workflow starts**<br>on its push, its schedule,<br>its release or by hand`"]

    push --> look
    look -->|yes| known
    look -->|no: those of a schedule, a release or a person| register
    register --> merge
    merge -->|a push that changes them registers them| unmark
    unmark --> again
    again --> known

    classDef bot fill:#526cfe2e,stroke:#526cfe,stroke-width:2px
    classDef person fill:#f59e0b2e,stroke:#f59e0b,stroke-width:2px
    classDef done fill:#16a34a2e,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    class look,register,unmark bot
    class push,merge,again person
    class known done
